    "id": "rk_02f64b70085c8c5532db",
    "text": "The centralization mandate (ch. 12) requires worship only at \"the place the LORD your God will choose\" — historically Jerusalem. Earlier narratives sometimes treat local altars as legitimate without the concern Deuteronomy 12 would lead a reader to expect: Elijah rebuilds Yahweh’s altar at Carmel (1 Kings 18:30), and Samuel offers sacrifices at several locations in 1 Samuel. Kings, however, does make cult centralization a major evaluative theme and portrays Hezekiah and Josiah as centralizing reformers. The compositional question is therefore not that no narrator ever notices non-centralized worship; it is how the earlier local-altars material, the later reform narratives, and Deuteronomy’s exclusive rule relate historically and literarily. CORRECTED 12 AUG 2026 ([Oxford Handbook discussion of centralization in Kings](https://academic.oup.com/edited-volume/57516/chapter-abstract/467904229))",
    "raw_text": "**The centralization mandate (ch. 12)** requires worship only at \"the place the LORD your God will choose\" — historically Jerusalem. Earlier narratives sometimes treat local altars as legitimate without the concern Deuteronomy 12 would lead a reader to expect: Elijah rebuilds Yahweh’s altar at Carmel (1 Kings 18:30), and Samuel offers sacrifices at several locations in 1 Samuel. Kings, however, does make cult centralization a major evaluative theme and portrays Hezekiah and Josiah as centralizing reformers. The compositional question is therefore not that no narrator ever notices non-centralized worship; it is how the earlier local-altars material, the later reform narratives, and Deuteronomy’s exclusive rule relate historically and literarily. `CORRECTED 12 AUG 2026` ([Oxford Handbook discussion of centralization in Kings](https://academic.oup.com/edited-volume/57516/chapter-abstract/467904229))",
