    "id": "rk_5c36da009c07ac0882ec",
    "text": "The Law of the King (17:14–20) requires the king to be Israelite, prohibits multiplying horses and wives, warns against returning to Egypt for horses, and requires personal study of the law. The former claim that the royal history ignores these standards does not survive comparison with Solomon: 1 Kings deliberately places his wealth, horses obtained through Egypt, and many foreign wives together, then says the wives turned his heart away—the danger Deuteronomy 17 names. That correspondence is evidence for a literary relationship between Deuteronomy and Kings; it is not evidence of “apparent divine tolerance.” The live historical question is the direction and stage of that relationship. CORRECTED 12 AUG 2026 ([Deuteronomy 17 and 1 Kings 10–11](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=deu+17%3A16-17%2C+1ki+10%3A14-29%2C+11%3A1-10&version=NIV))",
    "raw_text": "**The Law of the King (17:14–20)** requires the king to be Israelite, prohibits multiplying horses and wives, warns against returning to Egypt for horses, and requires personal study of the law. The former claim that the royal history ignores these standards does not survive comparison with Solomon: 1 Kings deliberately places his wealth, horses obtained through Egypt, and many foreign wives together, then says the wives turned his heart away—the danger Deuteronomy 17 names. That correspondence is evidence for a literary relationship between Deuteronomy and Kings; it is not evidence of “apparent divine tolerance.” The live historical question is the direction and stage of that relationship. `CORRECTED 12 AUG 2026` ([Deuteronomy 17 and 1 Kings 10–11](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=deu+17%3A16-17%2C+1ki+10%3A14-29%2C+11%3A1-10&version=NIV))",
